Here is an example of how you can create a Discord bot using Discord.js v14 with a single slash command, `ban`:
```
const { Client, SlashCommandBuilder } = require('discord.js');
const client = new Client();

client.commands = new Map();

client.login('YOUR_BOT_TOKEN_HERE');

client.on('ready', () => {
  console.log(`Logged in as ${client.user.tag}!`);
});

client.on('interactionCreate', async interaction => {
  if (!interaction.isCommand()) return;

  const command = client.commands.get(interaction.commandName);
  if (!command) return;

  try {
    await command.execute(interaction);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error(error);
    await interaction.reply({ content: 'There was an error while executing this command!', ephemeral: true });
  }
});

const banCommand = new SlashCommandBuilder()
 .setName('ban')
 .setDescription('Bans a user from the server.')
 .addUserOption(option => option.setName('user').setDescription('The user to ban.'))
 .addStringOption(option => option.setName('reason').setDescription('The reason for the ban.'));
client.commands.set('ban', {
  data: banCommand,
  execute: async (interaction) => {
    const user = interaction.options.getUser('user');
    const reason = interaction.options.getString('reason');

    // Check if the user has permission to ban
    if (!interaction.member.permissions.has('BAN_MEMBERS')) {
      return interaction.reply({ content: 'You do not have permission to ban members.', ephemeral: true });
    }

    // Check if the user is the bot
    if (user === interaction.client.user) {
      return interaction.reply({ content: 'You cannot ban the bot.', ephemeral: true });
    }

    // Perform the ban
    await interaction.guild.members.ban(user, { reason: reason });

    return interaction.reply({ content: `Successfully banned ${user.tag} for ${reason}.`, ephemeral: true });
  }
});

client.on('guildMemberRemove', async member => {
  // Check if the member was banned
  if (member.banned) {
    // Unban the member
    await member.ban({ reason: 'Unbanned' });
  }
});
```
This code creates a Discord bot with a single slash command, `ban`, which takes two options: `user` and `reason`. The `user` option specifies the user to ban, and the `reason` option specifies the reason for the ban.

When the `ban` command is invoked, the bot checks if the user has permission to ban members, and if so, performs the ban. The bot also checks if the user is trying to ban itself, and if so, returns an error message.

The bot also listens for the `guildMemberRemove` event, which is triggered when a member leaves the server. In this case, the bot checks if the member was banned, and if so, unbans them.

Note that you'll need to replace `YOUR_BOT_TOKEN_HERE` with your actual bot token, which you can obtain from the Discord Developer Portal.

Here is an example of a high-quality testing framework in Cypress that utilizes Docker and captures screenshots:
```
// cypress/support/index.js
import { addMatchImageSnapshotCommand } from 'cypress-image-snapshot/command';
import { addCucumberPlugin } from '@cypress/cucumber';
import { addDockerPlugin } from 'cypress-docker';

export function setupNodeEvents(Cypress, config) {
  // Add the image snapshot command
  addMatchImageSnapshotCommand(Cypress);

  // Add the Cucumber plugin
  addCucumberPlugin(Cypress, config);

  // Add the Docker plugin
  addDockerPlugin(Cypress, config);
}

export function setupNodeServer(Cypress, config) {
  // Configure the Docker plugin
  Cypress.config('docker', {
    image: 'your-image-name',
    containerName: 'your-container-name',
  });
}
```

```
// cypress/integration/cucumber.feature
Feature: My Application
  As a user
  I want to test my application
  So that I can ensure it is working correctly

Scenario: I navigate to the home page
  Given I am on the home page
  When I interact with the page
  Then I should see the correct content
```

```
// cypress/integration/cucumber.spec.js
import { Given, When, Then } from '@cypress/cucumber';
import { addMatchImageSnapshotCommand } from 'cypress-image-snapshot/command';

Given('I am on the home page', () => {
  cy.visit('/');
});

When('I interact with the page', () => {
  // Perform any necessary interactions with the page
  cy.get('[data-test="my-element"]').click();
});

Then('I should see the correct content', () => {
  // Verify that the correct content is displayed
  cy.get('[data-test="my-content"]').should('be.visible');
});

Then('the page should match the snapshot', () => {
  // Use the image snapshot command to capture and save a screenshot
  cy.matchImageSnapshot('home-page');
});
```

```
// cypress.config.js
module.exports = {
  //... other configuration options...

  docker: {
    image: 'your-image-name',
    containerName: 'your-container-name',
  },

  imageSnapshot: {
    // Configure the image snapshot plugin
    save: true,
    thumbnailSize: 100,
  },
};
```

In this example, we've added the following plugins:

* `@cypress/cucumber` for Cucumber support
* `cypress-image-snapshot` for image snapshotting
* `cypress-docker` for Docker support

We've also configured the `docker` plugin to use a specific image and container name. In your `cypress.config.js` file, you'll need to update these values with your own Docker image and container name.

In our Cucumber feature file, we've defined a scenario that navigates to the home page, interacts with the page, and verifies that the correct content is displayed. We've also added a step to capture and save a screenshot of the page using the `matchImageSnapshot` command.

In our Cucumber step definition file, we've implemented the steps for each scenario. We're using the `visit` command to navigate to the home page, the `get` command to interact with the page, and the `should` command to verify that the correct content is displayed. We're also using the `matchImageSnapshot` command to capture and save a screenshot of the page.

By using Docker and image snapshotting, we can ensure that our tests are running in a consistent and controlled environment, and that we have a visual record of each test run.

To achieve this, you can use a library like `EasyMarkdown` which is a C++ Markdown parser. Here's an example of how you can do it:

Firstly, install the EasyMarkdown library using your package manager (e.g., `brew install easy-markdown` on macOS). Then, include the necessary headers and link against the library.

Here's a simple function that converts Markdown to HTML:

```cpp
#include <easy-markdown/easy-markdown.h>
#include <string>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    EasyMarkdown::Markdown markdownObj;
    EasyMarkdown::Html htmlObj;

    EasyMarkdown::Error error;
    std::string html = EasyMarkdown::convert(markdownObj, markdown, &error);
    if (!error) {
        return EasyMarkdown::convert(htmlObj, html, &error);
    } else {
        return "Failed to convert Markdown to HTML: " + error.what();
    }
}
```

This function uses the `EasyMarkdown::convert` function to convert the Markdown text to HTML. The first argument to `EasyMarkdown::convert` is a pointer to a Markdown object, the second argument is the Markdown text to convert, and the third argument is a pointer to an `Error` object that will be set if an error occurs during the conversion.

The function then uses the same approach to convert the resulting HTML to a more readable format. Note that the EasyMarkdown library does not seem to provide a built-in way to format the HTML in a more readable way, so the resulting HTML will still be quite raw.

